  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWednesday-Thursday...A shortwave trough will swing into the base of \nthe longwave situated over the Upper Mississippi River Valley on \nWednesday, finally pushing the cold front further southeast\nalong with the scattered showers and storms. Areas that \nreceive repeated rounds of storms, especially after rainfall\nTuesday night, could see some localized flash flooding which is\nwhy the WPC has issued a Slight Risk (Level 2 out of 4) for \nexcessive rainfall for most of the CWA. By Wednesday night, a \nhigh pressure shifts into the CWA as convergence aloft continues\nto dry things out. Isolated showers and storms (20-30% \ncoverage) could stick around in northeast Missouri and west- \ncentral Illinois along an area of low-level isentropic upglide \nnear the baroclinic zone Thursday into Thursday night. \n\nFriday-Sunday...The longwave trough axis propagates into the Great \nLakes region, providing additional ageostrophic convergence aloft \nFriday into Saturday. As the high pressure over the region moves \neastward, southerly flow will likely return into the weekend and \nadvect in some warmer and more moist air. Highs could climb \nback into the upper 80s and low 90s by Sunday (50-80% chance per\n02.12z LREF run).",
